    <string name="courier">courier</string>
    <string name="welcome_text">You run a small courier office, and its only courier is you. The household places orders, you take the waybill out on a run, walk the shop in the order things lie on its shelves and bring it all home. Every run pays in brass tokens: a bicycle at the door, a sign over it, a van one day. No account, no internet, no ads.</string>
    <string name="how_it_works">How it works</string>
    <string name="open_office">Open the office</string>
    <string name="setup_title">Before the first run</string>
    <string name="setup_name">Who is the courier? Your name goes on the personnel file and on your own orders.</string>
    <string name="your_name">Your name</string>
    <string name="setup_currency">Prices and receipts are in:</string>
    <string name="setup_carry">How much do you carry comfortably on foot? A heavier waybill gets a warning.</string>
    <string name="setup_later">All of this can be changed in the settings.</string>
    <string name="g1_title">The waybill</string>
    <string name="g1_text">Everything home needs goes onto the waybill, each line with whose order it is. Share a list from a messenger to Errand and it is typed in line by line. A line starting with “!” is a rush: home within the hour pays extra.</string>
    <string name="g2_title">The run</string>
    <string name="g2_text">Out of the door, the waybill is sorted by the shop’s shelves. Each shop learns its own order from the way you tick things off; the cold box goes in last. The string bag fills up and shows how heavy it is getting. Shelf empty? Mark it, and the line rides over without a mark.</string>
    <string name="g3_title">Delivered</string>
    <string name="g3_text">At home, write the receipt and the weather and hand in the waybill. The payslip counts every line and every kilo, rushes on time, a run with nothing forgotten, a run within budget, rain and snow. The first thing bought without an order is your treat; more come off the pay.</string>
    <string name="g4_title">The depot</string>
    <string name="g4_text">Tokens are spent on the office: flowers in the window, an enamel sign, a clock, a lamp, then a bicycle, a cargo trike, a scooter and a van at the door. Ranks open bigger things: from errand runner up to director.</string>
    <string name="g5_title">The file and the notebook</string>
    <string name="g5_text">The personnel file keeps your rank, your record and the badges. The merchandiser’s notebook remembers prices: what went up since last month and which shop has it cheaper. The archive keeps every waybill with a report for each month.</string>
